   "Northwind-negotiator": "compromising",
   "Halcyon-researcher": "collaborating",
   "Halcyon-drafter": "competing",
   "Halcyon-negotiator": "compromising"
  },
  "holistic_F_N": 1,
  "holistic_F_H": -1,
  "section_notes": "All five sections land inside the pre-agreed acceptable ranges: S1 keeps strict 24-hour regulator reporting and 48-hour customer notice with 'all incidents' scope; S2 keeps the 55 °C test, a 24-month 7% transition to a permanent 5% cap with regulator-approved corrective plans, full per-model results to purchasers and regulators but no public publication; S3 requires new-unit shut-off with a 6-year retrofit window; S4 requires built-in suppression from 36 months or a validated equivalent; and S5 keeps a mandatory 8-per-100,000 recall trigger with 'promptly' notices and no phase-in. Numerically the outcome leans slightly toward Northwind (6-year retrofit, 7% interim cap, no S5 transition), while Halcyon secured customer-level per-model test disclosure and the 36-month suppression lead time."
 },
